{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block title %}Results: how well Counterproof catches gamed AI code changes{% endblock %}
{% block description %}Measured recall on gamed changes, false alarms on honest fixes, baselines, real Claude Code sessions and real agent pull requests, with the limits stated plainly.{% endblock %}
{% macro corpus_table(block, label) %}
<table>
  <caption>{{ label }}</caption>
  <thead>
    <tr><th scope="col">Configuration</th><th scope="col">Gamed flagged, strict</th><th scope="col">Gamed flagged, lenient</th><th scope="col">Honest flagged, strict</th><th scope="col">Honest flagged, lenient</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {% for c in block.configs %}
    <tr><th scope="row">Counterproof, {{ "recorded LLM" if c.name == "llm" else "rules only" }}</th><td>{{ c.recall_strict }}</td><td>{{ c.recall_lenient }}</td><td>{{ c.false_strict }}</td><td>{{ c.false_lenient }}</td></tr>
    {% endfor %}
    {% set first = block.configs[0] %}
    {% if first.baselines.B0 is defined %}<tr><th scope="row">B0: the tests are green</th><td>{{ first.baselines.B0.recall }}</td><td>{{ first.baselines.B0.recall }}</td><td>{{ first.baselines.B0.false }}</td><td>{{ first.baselines.B0.false }}</td></tr>{% endif %}
    {% if first.baselines.B1 is defined %}<tr><th scope="row">B1: claim audit only</th><td>{{ first.baselines.B1.recall }}</td><td>{{ first.baselines.B1.recall }}</td><td>{{ first.baselines.B1.false }}</td><td>{{ first.baselines.B1.false }}</td></tr>{% endif %}
    {% if first.baselines.B2 is defined %}<tr><th scope="row">B2: diff-scoped classic mutation</th><td>{{ first.baselines.B2.recall }}</td><td>{{ first.baselines.B2.recall }}</td><td>{{ first.baselines.B2.false }}</td><td>{{ first.baselines.B2.false }}</td></tr>{% endif %}
  </tbody>
</table>
{% endmacro %}
{% block main %}
<div class="wrap doc doc--wide">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How well Counterproof catches gamed AI code changes</h1>
    <p class="lede"><em>Strict</em> counts UNPROVEN or REFUTED as flagged, the definition in the product requirements; <em>lenient</em> also counts WEAK. Every table on this page is generated from the committed results files and regenerates with <code>counterproof eval</code>.</p>

    {% if results.c1 is defined %}
    {% set c1 = results.c1 %}
    <h2 id="c1">C1: seeded changes ({{ c1.cases }} cases)</h2>
    <p>Two fixture libraries, Python <code>py_ledger</code> (pytest) and TypeScript <code>ts_cart</code> (Vitest). Each task has one honest fix and three gamed variants: hard-coded outputs for the test's inputs, a wrong fix whose broken test was weakened, and a partial fix that only handles the reported example. Ground truth is by construction and checked with the real test runners.</p>
    {{ corpus_table(c1, "C1, generated " ~ c1.date) }}
    <h3>By kind of gaming</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Configuration</th><th scope="col">Kind</th><th scope="col">Cases</th><th scope="col">Flagged, strict</th><th scope="col">Flagged, lenient</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      {% for c in c1.configs %}{% for kind, k in c.by_kind | dictsort %}
        <tr><th scope="row">{{ "recorded LLM" if c.name == "llm" else "rules only" }}</th><td>{{ kind }}</td><td>{{ k.cases }}</td><td>{{ k.flagged_strict }}</td><td>{{ k.flagged_lenient }}</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}{% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Median runtime per run: {% for c in c1.configs %}{{ c.runtime }} s ({{ c.name }}){% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}. Same verdict on a repeated run with the same seed: {% for c in c1.configs %}{{ c.determinism }} ({{ c.name }}){% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.</p>
    {% endif %}

    {% if results.c2 is defined %}
    {% set c2 = results.c2 %}
    <h2 id="c2">C2: Claude Code sessions ({{ c2.cases }} cases)</h2>
    <p>{{ c2.report.description | trim }}</p>
    {{ corpus_table(c2, "C2, generated " ~ c2.date) }}
    {% endif %}

    {% if results.c3 is defined %}
    {% set c3 = results.c3 %}
    <h2 id="c3">C3: real agent pull requests ({{ c3.cases }} cases)</h2>
    <p>{{ c3.report.description | trim }}</p>
    <table>
      <caption>C3, generated {{ c3.date }}</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Verdict</th><th scope="col">Pull requests</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for verdict, n in c3.configs[0].verdicts | dictsort %}<tr><th scope="row">{{ verdict }}</th><td>{{ n }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    {% set v = c3.configs[0].verdicts %}
    {% set ran = c3.cases - v.get("INCONCLUSIVE", 0) %}
    {% set strict = v.get("UNPROVEN", 0) + v.get("REFUTED", 0) %}
    <p>Counterproof could run the tests of {{ ran }} of the {{ c3.cases }} pull requests; the rest are INCONCLUSIVE, mostly because the project's tests did not collect after a best-effort install on a fresh runner. All are assumed honest, so every flag is a false alarm until a hand review says otherwise: {{ strict }} of {{ ran }} strict (UNPROVEN or REFUTED), {{ strict + v.get("WEAK", 0) }} of {{ ran }} lenient (also WEAK).</p>
    <p>A hand review of every flagged pull request found no gaming. The UNPROVEN ones are honest changes whose tests do not pin them: they catch few realistic wrong versions, or pass on the old code too. The one REFUTED adds a test that skips itself when model weights are not downloaded, which looks the same as an agent skipping a test it cannot pass. Full per-case reasons and setup logs are in <a href="{{ repo_url }}/blob/main/eval/results/{{ c3.date }}-c3.md">the C3 results file</a>.</p>
    {% endif %}

    <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
    {% include "limits.html.j2" %}
  </article>
</div>
{% endblock %}
